      "section_breaks_from_first_tool_output": [
        {
          "before": "Observation shows that as the angle of incidence increases, the angle of refraction also increases.\nHowever, the two angles are not directly proportional.",
          "after": "Experiments in the seventeenth century established the law of refraction: **the incident ray, the refracted ray, and the normal to the boundary at the point of incidence lie in one plane; for a given pair of media, the ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is constant.**\nTo use this constant, keep the light's color and the physical states of both media unchanged."
        },
        {
          "before": "Table 9 gives the refractive indices of some substances relative to air, for yellow light.",
          "after": "A transparent semicylindrical glass block with a frosted back can be used to test the law of refraction.\nMount it on a graduated disk so that the center of the semicylindrical block coincides with the disk's center (Figure 155).\nLet the light travel along a radius of the disk and refract at the block's flat boundary; the frosted back makes the path visible."
        },
        {
          "before": "At normal incidence, $\\alpha=0$; the ray enters the second medium without bending, so $\\beta=0$.",
          "after": "This experiment also shows that the refracted beam does not carry all the incident light's energy.\nSome light is reflected at the boundary and returns to the first medium, air."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
